<p>
Bozhidar Batsov has been blogging up a storm on recent additions to Emacs that make our editing sessions easier. His latest post is about <a href="https://emacsredux.com/blog/2026/03/03/expreg-expand-region-reborn/">a successor to expand-region, expreg</a>. Batsov has been a heavy user of <a href="https://github.com/magnars/expand-region.el">expand-region</a> for many years. The problem with it is that it requires hand written functions for each language that it supports.
</p>
<p>
As a result of his recent work integrating <a href="https://emacs-tree-sitter.github.io/">Tree-sitter</a> into many of his packages, he thought it would make sense to reimplement expand-region as a Tree-sitter based package so that bespoke functions wouldn&rsquo;t have to be written for each language. It turns out that Yuan Fu, who implemented the Emacs builtin Tree-sitter, had already done it.
</p>
<p>
Batsov loves <a href="https://github.com/casouri/expreg">expreg</a> and doesn&rsquo;t see why anyone who isn&rsquo;t depending on some artifact of expand-region wouldn&rsquo;t adopt it. Take a look at his post for more details.
</p>
<p>
In the comments over at the Emacs subreddit <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/emacs/comments/1rjrjpy/comment/o8g0cpu/">Karthink</a> says that he has a constellation of packages that depend on expand-region and that it would be very hard for him to change. <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/emacs/comments/1rjrjpy/comment/o8g0cpu/">Spartanork</a> says that he had a hard time getting Tree-sitter installed. Those two comments suggest that expreg may not be for everyone.
</p>
<p>
I&rsquo;ve had expand-region installed for a long time but I don&rsquo;t use it as much as I should. At this point in my life, virtually all my coding is done in either Elisp or C, for which expand-region works well. It also does the right thing in a prose buffer so I see no reason to change but if you&rsquo;re using a lot of languages, you should definitely check out expreg.</p>
